class:: TTruncNormalRand
summary:: Triggered true truncated-normal sampler
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>Distributions
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/TGaussRand

description::

The Truncated Normal distribution generates values according to a standard Gaussian bell curve, but strictly rejects any values that fall outside specified minimum and maximum bounds. Conceptually, this provides the natural, clustered randomness of standard white or Gaussian noise while guaranteeing the output is absolutely safe for bounded parameter modulation. It allows for natural randomization of sensitive DSP parameters (like filter resonance or delay feedback) without risking mathematical blowups.

Samples a normal distribution conditioned on code::lo <= x <= hi:: by mapping an open uniform variate through the truncated CDF interval and Acklam's inverse-normal approximation. It does not fold or clip an ordinary normal draw.

Like link::Classes/TGaussRand::, it exposes normal mean and deviation; unlike TGaussRand, every result lies in an explicit interval and the conditioned density is preserved.

subsection:: Conditioned density

image::truncated-normal-density.png#An ordinary standard-normal density in muted teal and the true density conditioned to [-1, 1] in blue. Probability outside the interval is removed and the density inside is renormalized, not clipped or folded.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: mean
Untruncated normal mean.
argument:: deviation
Normal deviation; magnitude is used. Zero returns clipped mean.
argument:: lo
Lower boundary; reversed boundaries are exchanged.
argument:: hi
Upper boundary.
argument:: trig
Resample on a rising edge.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Restore the seed and sample a new initial value.
argument:: mul
Output multiplier.
argument:: add
Output offset.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with the same arguments.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ TTruncNormalRand.kr(0, 1, -0.5, 0.5, Impulse.kr(5)).poll }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ SinOsc.ar(TTruncNormalRand.kr(800, 500, 100, 3000, Impulse.kr(12)), 0, 0.08) }.play
)
::

code::
// Safe Bounded Parameter Modulation
(
{
    var trig, cutoff, sig;
    trig = Impulse.kr(12);
    // A true bell curve, but strictly clamped to avoid blowing up the resonant filter
    cutoff = TTruncNormalRand.kr(mean: 1000, deviation: 500, lo: 200, hi: 3000, trig: trig);
    sig = RLPF.ar(WhiteNoise.ar, cutoff, 0.05);
    Pan2.ar(LeakDC.ar(sig * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.01, 0.1), trig) * 0.5))
}.play;
)
::
